><p><strong>Cam Ward</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 BAL allow <strong>257 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p><strong>Wan'Dale Robinson</strong> draws a plus matchup \u2014 BAL concede <strong>74 yds/game to WR1s</strong> (league ~58). A rec-yards spot.</p><p>Sneaky value on <strong>Rashod Bateman</strong> (WR2) \u2014 TEN leak 44 yds/game to the WR2 role, above the ~36 norm.</p><p><strong>Tony Pollard</strong> eats as a receiver too \u2014 BAL give up 22 rec yds/game to backs, and TEN target the RB on 18% of throws.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Derrick Henry</strong> is a bell-cow (76% of carries) meeting a defense that allows 0.53 RB rushing TDs/game \u2014 strong anytime-TD equity.</p><p><strong>Zay Flowers</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 TEN surrender 0.53 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Script leans BAL.</strong> As a 8-point favorite they project to lead much of the game, tilting toward the run late \u2014 <strong>Derrick Henry</strong>'s carry ceiling rises in the blowout branches. Meanwhile TEN throw to keep pace, a volume tailwind for <strong>Wan'Dale Robinson</strong>.</p><p>A middling 48 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>BAL \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>BAL are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>30%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 8-point favorite projected to lead for 39% of snaps, <strong>BAL</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>Derrick Henry</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>28 carries for 135 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as BAL salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <strong>Zay Flowers</strong> (10 targets, 95 yards) has his ceiling <em>capped</em> by the same script that helps the run game \u2014 when BAL lead and run, there simply aren't as many pass attempts to go around. He's a safer floor than ceiling play in this spot.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Mark Andrews</strong> (TE, 7 targets, 55 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>TEN \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>TEN are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>38%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 TEN project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Tony Pollard</strong>'s rushing projection (15 carries, 68 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Wan'Dale Robinson</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, TEN funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>10 targets for 63 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Gunnar Helm</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 32 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark any